(;GM[1]FF[4]CA[UTF-8]SZ[19]KM[0]RU[Japanese]EV[Game with the Meijin (sponsored by the Yomiuri Shinbun for its 20,000th issue)]DT[1933-10-16,1934-01-29]PC[Tokyo]PB[Go Seigen]BR[5d]PW[Honinbo Shusai]WR[9d]RE[W+2]SO[Move list from Andries Brouwer's game archive, https://homepages.cwi.nl/~aeb/go/games/games/Go_Seigen/1933-10-16.sgf (the file credits GoGoD95), fetched 2026-09-18 and checked against Wikipedia, "Go Seigen" (section on the game against Honinbo Shusai): Go Seigen Black, Shusai White, started 16 October 1933, White wins by two points, Black's first three moves 3-3, 4-4 and tengen, the famous White move is move 160. 252 moves. Comments are Go Master's own.]C[Go Seigen (Black, 5 dan, aged 19) against Honinbo Shusai Meijin (White, 9 dan), sponsored by the Yomiuri newspaper for its 20,000th issue. Played in fourteen sessions from 16 October 1933 to 29 January 1934; no komi, as was usual then. Use the arrows or the ← → keys to step through.];B[qc]C[R17, the 3-3 point. Black's first three moves — 3-3, 4-4 and the centre point — had, Wikipedia says, never before been seen together in a professional game. This game is remembered for that opening as much as for its result.];W[cd]C[C16, a 3-4 point for White.];B[dp]C[D4, the 4-4 point — the star point in the lower left.];W[pq]C[Q3, a 3-4 point for White's second corner.];B[jj]C[K10: tengen, the centre of the board. The third of the three opening moves that made the game famous. A stone here claims nothing yet; it is a bet that the centre will matter in every fight to come.];W[pd]C[Q16, directly above Black's 3-3 stone. White presses the corner from the outside.];B[qd]C[R16: Black stands up alongside the white stone.];W[pe];B[ob];W[qn];B[jp]C[K4: Black's fourth stone on the lower side, level with the tengen stone. With D4 and K10 the black stones are spread across the whole board rather than concentrated in corners.];W[lq];B[dj]C[D10: another stone on a side star point. By move 13 Black has stones at D4, D10, K4 and K10 — a framework across the left and bottom, all of it fourth line or higher.];W[ed];B[lo];W[nq];B[kq];W[lp];B[ko];W[ch]C[C12: White begins to take the left side underneath Black's high stones. The question of the whole game is whether Black's centre will be worth more than the sides and corners White is collecting.];B[gj];W[fq];B[fo];W[iq];B[gp];W[cq];B[cp];W[dq];B[nd];W[ph];B[pj];W[ne];B[me];W[mf];B[nf];W[ng];B[oe]C[P15: the first capture of the game, a single white stone on the right side.];W[of];B[ne];W[nj];B[om];W[mg];B[ke];W[hc];B[qm];W[rm];B[rn];W[rl];B[ro];W[pm];B[ql];W[qk];B[pl];W[pn];B[pk];W[qj];B[nl];W[pi];B[qp];W[on]C[R4: after sixty moves both sides have a settled group on the right and the fight has moved to the lower side. Black's centre is still open.];B[nn];W[no];B[mo];W[op];B[qr];W[qq];B[rq];W[pr];B[rr];W[mn];B[nm];W[lm];B[ll];W[kp];B[mp];W[mq];B[ip];W[jq];B[kk];W[cn];B[ep];W[cj];B[ck];W[dk];B[cl];W[dl];B[ci];W[bj];B[bi];W[di];B[ej];W[bh];B[dm];W[ei];B[fj];W[cm];B[ak];W[ai]C[A11: White captures two stones on the left edge — the left side, which White had been undermining since move 20, is now White's.];B[el];W[bp];B[rf];W[co];B[qg];W[rh];B[jc];W[gq];B[eq];W[en];B[fn];W[er];B[fr];W[dr];B[hq];W[hr];B[hp];W[gr];B[fc];W[ic];B[dc];W[cc];B[gd];W[he];B[ge];W[je];B[hd];W[id];B[hf];W[kd];B[le];W[kc];B[og];W[oh];B[pg];W[em];B[ek]C[E9: Black captures two white stones in the lower left, in the fight that had been running there since move 100.];W[fm];B[gm];W[mb];B[cb];W[ec];B[eb];W[dd];B[db];W[gb];B[bc];W[bd];B[bb];W[nb];B[oa];W[fb];B[ea];W[gh];B[jf];W[ie];B[ff];W[hi];B[hg];W[eo];B[fp]C[F4: after 159 moves Black's stones stretch through the whole lower half of the board, and the centre-left is nearly enclosed.];W[hl]C[H8, White's 160th move: a stone dropped into the middle of the black area on the lower-left. Wikipedia calls it the brilliant move that in a single stroke brought White back into the game, and records the long-standing rumour that Shusai's student Maeda Nobuaki, not Shusai, found it during one of the many adjournments. Whoever found it, watch what it does to the area around it over the next forty moves.];B[il]C[J8: Black answers at once, blocking the stone from the right.];W[jh];B[fh];W[fi];B[hj];W[if];B[ih];W[ii];B[ig];W[jg];B[ji];W[fg];B[kf];W[ij];B[ik];W[hh];B[gf];W[mk];B[df];W[ml];B[sm];W[sk];B[mm];W[dg];B[li];W[ol];B[ln];W[nk];B[km];W[fl];B[gl];W[oj];B[qh];W[ri];B[cf];W[ef];B[gi];W[eh];B[ee];W[eg]C[E15: the fight that started with move 160 has spread upwards through the whole centre. Compare this position with the one at move 159: the black area on the lower-left has been cut into pieces.];B[de];W[bf];B[bq];W[br];B[ps];W[os];B[qs];W[oq];B[bo];W[aq];B[an];W[bn];B[am];W[ao];B[rg];W[mi];B[qi];W[lh];B[kh];W[lj];B[rj];W[rk];B[sh];W[sj];B[bg];W[ae];B[cg];W[ag];B[fe];W[gc];B[fd];W[ce];B[kg]C[L13: Black captures two stones in the centre; the last fight of the game.];W[mc];B[ki];W[fk];B[ld];W[lc];B[gk];W[bk];B[bl];W[na];B[nc];W[gg];B[fa];W[ga];B[ac];W[ad];B[lg];W[mh];B[qo];W[lk]C[M9, the last move. White wins by two points — Wikipedia's figure and the record's. Shusai adjourned the game more than a dozen times, always at his own turn; whether the winning move at 160 was his is still argued about. The three opening moves are what the game is remembered for, and Go Seigen went on to be the dominant player of the century.])
